; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Sierpinski Arrowhead
; CATEGORY: Fractals
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     recursion, arrowhead curve, parity, single path
; SUMMARY:  Draws the Sierpinski triangle as one unbroken wiggly path, the arrowhead curve.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   The usual Sierpinski triangle is drawn as many separate
;   triangles. The arrowhead curve traces the same shape in a single
;   stroke that never crosses itself. Each level is three half sized
;   copies, the outer two mirrored. The program shows levels 2, 4
;   and 6 small on the left, then level 8 big.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Read ARROWHEAD to see how one :turn input does the
;   work of two mirror image procedures.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * a :turn input whose sign flips a whole sub-curve
;   * turning at the start and undoing it at the end, so a sub-curve
;     behaves like one straight step from corner to corner
;   * reusing one procedure to draw at several sizes and places
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Draw levels 1, 3 and 5. What shape is level 1?
;   * Remove the first and last turn in ARROWHEAD and see it break.
;   * Change the 60 degree turns to 90. What curve appears?
;   * Color the three sub-curves of the top level differently.
; ============================================================

; ARROWHEAD draws the curve between two corners :len apart, starting
; and finishing with the turtle facing from the first corner to the
; second. :turn is 60 or -60; the two outer copies get the opposite
; turn, which mirrors them. (As an L-system: A -> B-A-B, B -> A+B+A.)
to arrowhead :len :level :turn
  if :level = 0 [forward :len stop]
  left :turn
  arrowhead :len / 2 :level - 1 minus :turn
  right :turn
  arrowhead :len / 2 :level - 1 :turn
  right :turn
  arrowhead :len / 2 :level - 1 minus :turn
  left :turn
end

; TRIANGLE.AT draws a level :level arrowhead with its base from
; (x, y) to (x + len, y). Because ARROWHEAD turns back at the end by
; as much as it turned at the start, the turtle always finishes
; facing east again, at the right hand corner.
to triangle.at :x :y :len :level :color
  penup setxy :x :y setheading 90 pendown
  setpencolor :color
  arrowhead :len :level 60
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [252 250 245]
setpensize 2
; Three small ones down the left side show how the curve grows...
triangle.at -380 160 130 2 [200 80 60]
triangle.at -380 10 130 4 [220 150 40]
triangle.at -380 -140 130 6 [60 150 90]
; ...and one big one fills the rest of the screen.
setpensize 1.2
triangle.at -170 -230 540 8 [40 80 170]
